Adding an example
-----------------

The rights question comes first, and "it is old" does not settle it. A term of the
author's life plus seventy years is only the starting point: France adds thirty years
for an author who died for it (art. L123-10 CPI), and the United States runs
ninety-five years from publication for anything published between 1929 and 1977. A
work can therefore be public domain across most of Europe and still in copyright in
both France and the United States — which is exactly the case for Le Petit Prince,
and the reason it is not one of these examples. Prefer an author who died before 1930
whose work was also published before 1930: that clears every term at once.


